   "en": {
    "headline": "US voters push back against AI data centers in the midterm elections",
    "summary_short": "Local measures against data centers won almost all their votes, and Texas will be governed by a Democrat who wants a moratorium. Several AI-linked stocks fell as the results came in.",
    "summary_long": "In the midterm elections, about 40 localities in 13 states voted on data centers. According to the Data Centers on the Ballot tally, 33 measures to restrict them have already passed and only one has failed. In an NBC News poll, 76 % of voters opposed having a data center in their community. The biggest blow comes in Texas, the fastest-growing market: Democrat Gina Hinojosa, who proposes an immediate moratorium, won the governorship. Wells Fargo estimates that the state holds 27 % of the data center gigawatts planned in the US.\n\nThe stock market reacted: SpaceX fell 4 %, Tesla 5 % and CoreWeave and Iren 4 %, although Alphabet, Amazon and Microsoft rose. Halting projects will not be simple. In Texas, cities, counties and the state all have a say, and construction unions backed some of the winners. Also, of the more than 300 local bans already enacted, only three have delayed projects.",
    "unconfirmed_claims": []
   },
